This version keeps the current values in a vector and uses `nth_element` after every insertion. It places the `k`th largest value at position `size - k`, without sorting every item around it.

It is a useful alternative when a full heap is not wanted, but it is slower for a long stream because the vector keeps growing and the partition work is repeated. The input vector is copied, so `nums` is left unchanged.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class KthLargest {
private:
    int k;
    vector<int> values;

public:
    KthLargest(int k, vector<int>& nums) : k(k), values(nums) {
        if (static_cast<int>(values.size()) >= k) {
            int target = static_cast<int>(values.size()) - k;
            nth_element(values.begin(), values.begin() + target, values.end());
        }
    }

    int add(int val) {
        values.push_back(val);
        int target = static_cast<int>(values.size()) - k;
        nth_element(values.begin(), values.begin() + target, values.end());
        return values[target];
    }
};
```

After `k = 3` and `[4, 5, 8, 2]`, the target position is `1`, so the value at that position is `4`. Adding `10` changes the target to `2`, and the value there becomes `5`.

`nth_element` has average `O(n)` work for one update, where `n` is the number of values currently stored. The constructor has average `O(n)` time, while repeated calls can become expensive. Space complexity is `O(n)` because the complete stream is kept.

The heap below is written directly in Go because the standard library does not provide a ready-made integer min-heap. It stores at most `k` numbers. The root is the smallest of them, which is the answer we need.

The constructor calls `Add` for the initial values, so the setup and stream updates use the same rule.

```go
package main

import "container/heap"

type intMinHeap []int

func (h intMinHeap) Len() int {
	return len(h)
}

func (h intMinHeap) Less(i, j int) bool {
	return h[i] < h[j]
}

func (h intMinHeap) Swap(i, j int) {
	h[i], h[j] = h[j], h[i]
}

func (h *intMinHeap) Push(value interface{}) {
	*h = append(*h, value.(int))
}

func (h *intMinHeap) Pop() interface{} {
	old := *h
	last := len(old) - 1
	value := old[last]
	*h = old[:last]
	return value
}

type KthLargest struct {
	k    int
	heap *intMinHeap
}

func Constructor(k int, nums []int) *KthLargest {
	values := &intMinHeap{}
	heap.Init(values)

	result := &KthLargest{k: k, heap: values}
	for _, value := range nums {
		result.Add(value)
	}
	return result
}

func (kl *KthLargest) Add(val int) int {
	if kl.heap.Len() < kl.k {
		heap.Push(kl.heap, val)
	} else if val > (*(kl.heap))[0] {
		heap.Pop(kl.heap)
		heap.Push(kl.heap, val)
	}
	return (*(kl.heap))[0]
}
```

For example, with `k = 2` and values `[5, 1, 9]`, the heap keeps `[5, 9]`, so the root is `5`. A later `add(3)` is ignored because it is not larger than the current boundary.

Each `Add` uses `O(log k)` time and the heap takes `O(k)` space. The constructor takes `O(n log k)` time for `n` initial values.

A `multiset` keeps values sorted and also handles repeated numbers. I keep only the largest `k` values in it. Since the smallest value in that group is the `k`th largest overall, `begin()` gives the result.

This is more memory-friendly than saving the whole stream, but a multiset has tree-node overhead compared with a compact heap.

```cpp
#include <functional>
#include <set>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class KthLargest {
private:
    int k;
    multiset<int> topValues;

public:
    KthLargest(int k, vector<int>& nums) : k(k) {
        for (int value : nums) {
            add(value);
        }
    }

    int add(int val) {
        topValues.insert(val);
        if (static_cast<int>(topValues.size()) > k) {
            topValues.erase(topValues.begin());
        }
        return *topValues.begin();
    }
};
```

Edge cases do not need special branches here. With `k = 1`, the set contains only the largest value. Negative values and equal values work normally.

The constructor takes `O(n log k)` time when it processes `n` starting values. Each `add` call takes `O(log k)`, and the stored set has `O(k)` space.

Only the largest `k` values matter. A min-heap stores those values, and its smallest item is the current `k`th largest value.

For each initial number and each later value, the code adds it to the heap. If the heap grows past `k`, the smallest item is removed. Duplicate numbers stay in the heap because they count separately.

```java
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

class KthLargest {
    private final int k;
    private final PriorityQueue<Integer> heap;

    public KthLargest(int k, int[] nums) {
        this.k = k;
        this.heap = new PriorityQueue<>();

        for (int value : nums) {
            add(value);
        }
    }

    public int add(int val) {
        heap.offer(val);
        if (heap.size() > k) {
            heap.poll();
        }
        return heap.peek();
    }
}
```

A small dry run with `k = 3` and `[4, 5, 8, 2]` leaves `[4, 5, 8]` in the heap after setup. Adding `10` makes four items, so `4` is removed and the new answer is `5`.

Each insertion or removal costs `O(log k)`. Building the object costs `O(n log k)` for `n` initial values. The heap uses `O(k)` space.

